  "title": "Roland TR-808 Rhythm Composer",
  "year": "1980",
  "subtitle": "The 16-button step-sequencer grid that taught a generation to think in patterns",
  "why_scout_cares": "The TR-808 introduced the step-sequencer grid as a physical interaction paradigm — sixteen buttons with LEDs, one per sixteenth-note step, where composing rhythm becomes an act of button-toggling. Nothing else in the museum uses a grid-based pattern programming interface. The 808 was a commercial failure (~12,000 units) that became the most influential drum machine in history, foundational to hip-hop and electronic music. The HCI story isn't the sounds; it's the physical interface that transformed how humans program rhythm.",
  "search_terms": ["Roland TR-808", "drum machine", "step sequencer", "grid interface", "pattern programming", "1980"],
